# Prometheus discover

## Gate

Use when the user asks which metrics/labels exist, what a job scrapes, target
health, series shape, cardinality inventory, or needs names before a query.
Skip bare "Prometheus" with no discover/query ask. Load `prometheus-safety`
with this rule when admin/config tools may run.

Never invent a metric name, label name, job, or instance — resolve from MCP
this turn or from a name the user pasted.

## Prefer MCP

When a Prometheus MCP is ready (`user-mcp_prometheus`), use these tools —
do not invent a side CLI or raw HTTP client while MCP works:

| Need | Tool | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| List metric names | `label_values` | `label="__name__"`; optional `matches`, time window |
| Label names | `label_names` | optional `matches`, `start_time` / `end_time` |
| Label values | `label_values` | required `label`; optional `matches` |
| Series selectors | `series` | required `matches` (array of selectors) |
| Metric type/help | `metric_metadata` | optional `metric`, `limit` |
| Scrape targets | `list_targets` | up/down / health inventory |
| Target metric meta | `targets_metadata` | optional `match_target`, `metric` |
| Cardinality / TSDB | `tsdb_stats` | series counts, heavy labels |
| Alerts / rules | `list_alerts`, `list_rules` | inventory only here |
| Server health | `healthy`, `ready`, `build_info`, `runtime_info` | status |
| Scrape config read | `config`, `flags` | read-only inventory |
| Alertmanager discovery | `alertmanagers` | discovery overview |
| Docs (syntax) | `docs_search`, `docs_list`, `docs_read` | official Prom docs |

## Discover-before-query ladder

1. Prefer exact metric / job / instance the user named.
2. Else `label_values` with `label="__name__"` (and a pattern via `matches`
   when the surface is known, e.g. `{__name__=~".*cpu.*"}`).
3. `metric_metadata` on candidates to learn type (counter/gauge/histogram)
   and help text — counters need `rate()`, gauges usually do not.
4. `series` with `matches=["metric_name"]` (or a tighter selector) to see
   which labels exist on that metric.
5. `label_values` for key labels (`job`, `instance`, `namespace`, custom)
   before writing matchers — confirm spelling and case.
6. For scrape problems: `list_targets` first; then `targets_metadata` if you
   need what a target exposes.
7. For "why is this huge?": `tsdb_stats` → top series-by-metric / label
   value counts before guessing high-cardinality labels.
8. If several metrics match, list a short shortlist and ask once; do not
   pick a production metric by guess.

## Time windows on discovery

- `label_names`, `label_values`, and `series` accept `start_time` /
  `end_time` (Unix seconds, RFC3339, or relative like `1h`, `5m`).
- Default discovery windows are often short — widen when the metric is
  rare or the user named a historical window.
- Keep `truncation_limit` modest on first pulls (tens–low hundreds); raise
  only when the truncated answer is clearly incomplete.

## Targets and missing data

When metrics are missing or stale:

1. `list_targets` — look for down / unknown scrape health.
2. Confirm the metric still exists via `label_values` / `series`.
3. `targets_metadata` for what the target claims to expose.
4. Optional `config` to verify scrape jobs (read-only; do not rewrite
  scrape configs from chat unless the user asked for file edits).

## Report shape

- Metric / label names actually returned by MCP (not guessed)
- Short type/help note when `metric_metadata` was used
- Target job/instance + health when targets were the ask
- One recommended next step (query, tighter matchers, or cardinality check)

## Out of scope

- Instant/range PromQL execution → `prometheus-query`
- Dashboard / Explore UX on other products — own Prometheus MCP only
- Destructive TSDB admin (`delete_series`, `clean_tombstones`, `quit`) →
  `prometheus-safety` gate; never run as part of discovery
